From f67d389f495da7ae85d0b20f3d5ab527bcaa6560 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 22:59:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 20/21] l2r-local: entry 23-l: drop stack: runs of drops with
 one release function

Drops deferred one after the other with one release function form one
run: the run (release, last cell, count) is one entry, kept in the
thread's state while it is the top one, and each of its cells but the last
is one pointer in a second vector. A pending cell then takes 8 bytes while
it waits (4 on wasm32) instead of a 16-byte entry (the boxed heads of a
list that the glue frees whole, a host runtime's cells), and a deferral or
a pop that stays in the top run touches no vector but the cells'. A run of
one cell below the top takes a 24-byte entry (12 on wasm32) where the
entry was 16 bytes (8): drops that alternate release functions (the dead
children of two record types in turn) take half as much again while they
wait. The order is the same: last deferred first. Both buffers shrink back
to KEEP elements after a drain and are freed at thread exit, as before.
Runs form only between equal function pointers: a function reified twice
may get two addresses (Miri does that every time), which costs entries,
never order. Test: the entries and cells of three runs (the release
reified once), and their order.
